@import "./compiler.sly"
@use Build

class ProcessUtils {
    Runs a command, given as the words of its command line, with an optional working directory and an
    optional text handed over stdin, and returns its exit code, its stdout and its stderr once it exits.
    A command that cannot be spawned returns exit code 127 and the reason as its stderr.
}

class SlytherRuntime {
    How a deterministic step of a given lang is run, checked and installed. It is created with the lang and
    the folder the artifacts are built into, and throws when the lang is not one it knows. The langs are:

    - ts: run with `bun`, checked with `bun build --no-bundle`, dependencies in a package.json installed with
      `bun install`, ignoring node_modules
    - py: run with the python of the `.venv` of the artifacts folder, checked with `py_compile`, dependencies in
      a requirements.txt installed with `pip` into the `.venv`, which is created when missing, ignoring `.venv`
    - sh: run with `sh`, checked with `sh -n`, no dependencies

    Every command it returns has its paths relative to the root of the project, since that is where every
    step runs: a step prints paths relative to the root, so its working directory is the root.

    It gives the extension of a script of the lang, the command that runs a script, the command that checks
    its syntax, the file that declares the dependencies of every step of the lang, as a name and a content
    built from a `name: version` map, and the command that installs them, if the lang has any.
}

class SlytherGenerator {
    What writes the scripts and the markdown a step needs, as an abstract class every backend implements
    with two primitives: `ask`, which sends a prompt and returns a JSON reply that matches a schema, along
    with a session that a later `ask` may resume so a fix continues the same conversation, and `execute`,
    which runs a prompt with the tools to read and edit the project, for the llm steps of an operation.

    On top of them it defines `generate`, which takes a #{SlytherGenerationTask} and returns the files it
    asked for, the dependencies they declare and the session, and `fix`, which resumes the session with the
    reason the files failed and returns them corrected. Both throw when a reply lacks an expected file or
    adds one that was not asked for.
}

class SlytherGenerationTask {
    What a #{SlytherGenerator} is asked to write, contains:

    - instructions: The prose of what to write: the rules of the kind, the operation, the step, its params
      and its lang, and the conventions every script follows
    - context: The files the generator may need to read, each with its path and its content, gathered by
      Slyther beforehand so the build never depends on what a backend chooses to explore
    - expected: The paths of the files the reply must contain, relative to the root of the project
    - dependencies: The `name: version` map of what is already installed for the lang, so the reply
      reuses it instead of adding another version or another package that does the same
}

class ClaudeCLIGenerator {
    A #{SlytherGenerator} backed by the Claude Code CLI, `claude` unless the SLYTHER_CLAUDE environment
    variable names another executable. `ask` runs it in print mode with every tool disabled, the prompt
    on stdin, the JSON output format and the schema as its structured output, resuming the session when
    given one, and returns the structured output and the session id of the reply. `execute` runs it in
    print mode in the given directory with the tools to read, search and edit files and to run commands
    allowed, and returns the text of the reply. Either throws when the CLI does not reply, or replies with
    an error.
}

class SlytherVerifier {
    Checks that the script written for a deterministic step works before it is recorded as built, and
    returns why it does not, or nothing when it does. It is created with the root of the project.

    Every script is checked for syntax with the command of its #{SlytherRuntime}. The scripts of the
    read-only operations, locate, list, signature and uses, are also run, since running them changes
    nothing: list with no args must exit 0, and locate must find the first id it prints, so list prints
    the ids locate takes; the others must exit 0 or 1 given the first id list prints, or a sample id when
    it prints none, and print nothing when they exit 1. When signature exits 0, every line it prints must
    have the `key shape` form, and when uses exits 0, every line must have the `kind:id key` form.
}

class SlytherArtifactManifest {
    What a build remembers, written as manifest.json in the artifacts folder, contains:

    - operations: Every operation built, keyed by `kind::name`, with its kind, its name, whether it is
      deterministic, its params, the markdown that orchestrates it when it is not deterministic, and its
      steps in order, each with its name, its kind, its path relative to the artifacts folder and, for a
      deterministic one, its lang and the command that runs it
    - files: Every file built, keyed by its path relative to the artifacts folder, with the input hash it
      was built from, the output hash of the content it was written with, and the dependencies it declares

    Loading a manifest that does not exist gives an empty one. It is saved with its entries sorted, so it
    diffs well, and gives the union of the dependencies declared by every file of a lang, one version per
    package.
}

class SlytherArtifactBuilder {
    Brings the artifacts folder in line with a list of #{Compiler::SlytherArtifactKind}, given the root of
    the project, the folder relative to it and a #{SlytherGenerator}. Every kind with operations gets a
    folder per operation, `kind/operation`, holding a script per deterministic step, named after the step
    with the extension of its #{SlytherRuntime}, a markdown per llm step, and operation.md for an
    operation that is not deterministic. The operations locate, list, signature and uses are built before
    the others, since the others may run them.

    The input hash of a step is the hash of its closure hash, the scope hash of its operation, the scope
    hash of its kind, its lang and its path, so it changes when the step, the rules or the operation
    change and not when a sibling step does. The input hash of operation.md is the hash of the closure
    hash of the operation, the scope hash of the kind and its path.

    A file is built when the #{SlytherArtifactManifest} does not know it, when its input hash changed,
    when it is missing, or when the hash of its content is not the output hash it was written with,
    which means it was edited by hand. A file the manifest knows that no kind asks for anymore is
    removed, along with the folders left empty. Anything else is kept, and the build reports what it
    did to every file.

    The markdown of an llm step and operation.md are written by Slyther itself: the first holds the rules
    of the kind, the params of the operation and the prose of the step, the second lists the steps in
    order, as the command to run for a deterministic one, with every param as `"{name}"`, and as the
    markdown to follow for an llm one. A script is asked from the generator with a
    #{SlytherGenerationTask}: its instructions hold the rules of the kind, the operation and its params,
    the step, the conventions every script follows and the commands that run the other deterministic
    operations of the kind already built, and its context holds the prose of every artifact the kind, the
    operation or the step reference. Once written, the dependencies the reply declares are recorded, the
    dependencies file of the lang is rewritten when their union changed and installed, and the script is
    checked with #{SlytherVerifier}: when it fails, the failure is sent back with `fix`, up to three
    attempts, after which the build throws and the file is not recorded, so the next build tries again.

    The manifest is saved after every file is built, so a build that fails halfway keeps what it did, and
    a .gitignore inside the artifacts folder ignores whatever the runtimes in use create.
}
